# The ledger of `toml`: what TOML 1.1.0 (toml.io) asks of a reader and what
# Python's `tomllib` (3.15) lets a program do, one block per capability, and
# what Rebel answers. Neither Go's nor Rust's standard library reads TOML, so
# their lines are `-` in every block; Python's reads and does not write. The
# list is this machine's, 2026-11-16: `dir(tomllib)`,
# `dir(tomllib.TOMLDecodeError)`, the signatures `inspect.signature` prints,
# and an instance's attributes. Every expected value of the package's tests
# that tomllib answers was observed from it, tomllib 3.15.2 for the forms
# TOML 1.1.0 adds (2026-11-19).
#
# TOML 1.1.0, not 1.0.0, since 2026-11-19: no mode reads or writes 1.0.0
# alone. What 1.1.0 changes, read from the specification's text, its ABNF
# and its changelog at the toml-lang/toml repository's tag `1.1.0`
# (2025-12-18), each in the specification's words, then what `toml` does:
#
# 1. Inline tables across lines, with comments, and a comma after the last
#    member. "Inline tables can have multiple key/value pairs on the same
#    line, or they can be put on different lines. A terminating comma (also
#    called trailing comma) is permitted after the last key/value pair."; the
#    ABNF: `inline-table-keyvals = ws-comment-newline keyval
#    ws-comment-newline [ inline-table-sep ]`. Read by `parse`, and edited in
#    place by `set_value`, `remove_value` and `add_table`: a member added on
#    a line of its own where the last stands on its own, its last comma kept;
#    one removed with its line and its comment. `format` writes an inline
#    table on one line. A line end inside a member, between its key, its `=`
#    and its value, is still refused, as the ABNF and tomllib refuse it.
# 2. The escape `\xHH`. "\xHH - unicode (U+00HH)"; "Any Unicode character
#    may be escaped with the `\xHH`, `\uHHHH`, or `\UHHHHHHHH` forms." Read,
#    hexadecimal digits of either case, `\x00` included; `format` writes
#    every control character with no short escape as `\xHH`, where it wrote
#    `\u00HH`.
# 3. The escape `\e`. "\e - escape (U+001B)". Read; `format` writes ESC as
#    `\e`.
# 4. Seconds left out of a date and time with an offset. "One exception to
#    RFC 3339 is permitted: seconds may be omitted, in which case `:00` will
#    be assumed. The offset immediately follows the minutes." Read as `:00`.
# 5. Seconds left out of a local date and time. "Seconds may be omitted, in
#    which case `:00` will be assumed." Read as `:00`.
# 6. Seconds left out of a local time. "Seconds may be omitted, in which case
#    `:00` will be assumed." Read as `:00`. In 4 to 6 a fraction follows only
#    seconds, `partial-time = time-hour ":" time-minute [ ":" time-second [
#    time-secfrac ] ]`, so `07:32.5` is refused, as tomllib refuses it;
#    `format` and `to_json` write every time with its seconds, the form both
#    versions and RFC 3339 read.
# 7. Comments. "Parsers must not modify keys or values, based on the
#    presence (or contents) of a comment." Held already: a comment reaches no
#    value.
# 8. Unicode and UTF-8. "Specifically this means that a file _as a whole_
#    must form a well-formed code-unit sequence. Otherwise, it must be
#    rejected (preferably) or have ill-formed byte sequences replaced with
#    U+FFFD"; "All strings must contain only Unicode characters." Held
#    already: a `str` is UTF-8, `read_file` fails `not_text`, and an escape of
#    no Unicode scalar value is refused.
# 9. Where dotted keys define tables. "Any such table must have all its
#    key/value pairs defined under the current `[table]` header, or in the
#    root table if defined before all headers, or in one inline table." Held
#    already: a dotted key adds no key, past the next header, to a table it
#    defined (block toml/tables).
# 10. Line ends in a multi-line literal string. "TOML parsers must normalize
#    newlines in the same manner as multi-line basic strings." Held already:
#    CR LF is read as LF in both. And "Carriage returns (U+000D) are only
#    allowed as part of a newline sequence": a lone CR refused, as before.
# 11. The precision of a fraction of a second. "Implementations are required
#    to support at least millisecond precision. Additional digits of
#    precision may be specified, but if they exceed the supported precision
#    then the extra digits must be truncated, not rounded." Held already:
#    nine digits kept, the rest cut.
# 12. The sizes of numbers. "Implementations are free to support any integer
#    size. It's recommended that at least 64-bit signed integers (from -2^63
#    to 2^63-1) are accepted and handled losslessly. If an integer cannot be
#    represented losslessly, an error must be thrown."; "Implementations are
#    free to support any precision level. It's recommended that at least IEEE
#    754 binary64 values are supported." Held already: an `i64`, failing
#    `does_not_fit` past it, and an `f64`.
# 13. DEL in a comment. The ABNF's `non-eol = %x09 / %x20-7E / non-ascii`,
#    where 1.0.0's said `%x20-7F` and its text refused U+007F. Held already:
#    DEL refused in a comment, as both texts say.
# 14. A multi-line string as a key. "You cannot use multi-line strings to
#    define quoted keys." Held already, 1.0.0's ABNF saying the same.
#
# Measured on 2026-11-19 with toml-test (BurntSushi/toml-test at 96fd0884,
# its own runner built from it), a decoder and an encoder built on this
# package in the scratch folder, `-toml 1.1.0`: valid 218 passed and 0
# failed, invalid 502 and 0, the encoder 218 and 0; before this change the
# same decoder read valid 207 and 11 failed, invalid 502 and 0. Under
# `-toml 1.0.0` valid 208 and 0, the encoder 208 and 0, and invalid 500 and
# 9: the nine cases 1.0.0 refuses and 1.1.0 accepts, the flips, which read
# 509 and 0 before: `invalid/datetime/no-secs`, `invalid/local-datetime/no-secs`,
# `invalid/local-time/no-secs`, `invalid/string/basic-byte-escapes`,
# `invalid/inline-table/trailing-comma` and `invalid/inline-table/linebreak-01`
# to `-04`. Every valid 1.1.0 case, read by `parse` and written by `format`,
# reads back in tomllib 3.15.2 as tomllib reads the case itself: 218 of 218.

capability toml/parse  a TOML text read into a tree of values a program matches on
  heroes  toml.parse, toml.Value
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tomllib.loads, dict, list, str, int, float, bool, datetime.datetime, datetime.date, datetime.time
  note    a document is a `table`; its members keep the order they were defined in, as a Python dict does; ten cases, one per kind TOML names

capability toml/load  a TOML file read
  heroes  built-in read_file, toml.parse
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tomllib.load
  note    the program reads the file, failing `not_text` where it is not UTF-8, and parses its text

capability toml/strings  the four forms of string and their escapes
  heroes  toml.parse
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tomllib.loads
  note    basic, literal, and each across lines, up to two quotes before the closing three, a backslash ending a line; CR LF inside a string read as LF, as tomllib reads it. `\e` and `\xHH`, TOML 1.1.0's, read as tomllib reads them, `\x00` and `\xE9` included, and `\E`, `\X41` and `\x4` refused as it refuses them (refused here until 2026-11-19, 1.0.0 then followed); a control character, a lone CR and an escape of no Unicode scalar value are refused as both refuse them

capability toml/integers  integers in decimal, hexadecimal, octal and binary, with underscores
  heroes  toml.parse, toml.DOES_NOT_FIT
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tomllib.loads
  note    an integer past an `i64` fails `does_not_fit`, as TOML 1.0.0 requires; tomllib reads it, a Python integer having no bound. A leading zero, a sign before a prefix, `0X` and a lone or doubled underscore are refused as tomllib refuses them

capability toml/floats  floats with a fraction, an exponent, underscores, `inf` and `nan`
  heroes  toml.parse
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tomllib.loads
  note    read as the nearest `f64`; an underscore between the exponent's digits is read, as tomllib reads it

capability toml/float-reader  floats read by a function of the program's, a decimal type
  heroes  refused: a float is the nearest `f64`, and a reader inside the parse would need a function value and a failure of its own; a program that wants a float's digits writes the value as a string
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tomllib.loads(parse_float=), tomllib.load(parse_float=)
  note    -

capability toml/dates  dates and times in their four forms, as `time` values
  heroes  toml.Value, time.DateTime, time.Duration
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  datetime.datetime, datetime.date, datetime.time, datetime.timezone
  note    a date and time with an offset is held as written, with its offset in minutes, so that every year from 0 to 9999 is held; a fraction keeps nine digits, the rest cut, where tomllib keeps six. A time with no seconds, TOML 1.1.0's, in the three forms that hold a time, is read as `:00`, as tomllib reads it (refused here until 2026-11-19), and a fraction with no seconds before it refused as tomllib refuses it; a second of 60 is refused, as tomllib refuses it, `time` having no leap second; the year 0 is read, as RFC 3339 allows, where tomllib refuses it for Python's calendar

capability toml/instant  the instant a date and time with an offset names
  heroes  toml.instant_of
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  datetime.datetime.timestamp, datetime.datetime.astimezone
  note    fails `does_not_fit` outside what a `time.Instant` holds, 1677 to 2262

capability toml/tables  tables, arrays of tables, dotted keys and inline tables, with the rules of their definition
  heroes  toml.parse
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tomllib.loads
  note    a header names a table once, a super-table may follow its child, a dotted key adds no key to a table a header defined nor, past the next header, to one it defined itself, and an inline table or an array written whole takes nothing more: each refused as tomllib refuses it, the message naming the table's whole path

capability toml/inline-tables-1.1  an inline table across lines, with comments, or with a comma before its end
  heroes  toml.parse, toml.set_value, toml.remove_value, toml.add_table
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tomllib.loads
  note    TOML 1.1.0's, read as tomllib reads them since 2026-11-19, refused until then; `{,}`, `{ x = 1,, }` and a line end between a key, its `=` and its value refused as tomllib refuses them. A document edited in place keeps such a table's layout: a member added on a line of its own where the last stands on its own, a last comma kept, one removed with its line and its comment; `format` writes an inline table on one line

capability toml/errors  a text that is not TOML, with its line and column
  heroes  toml.INVALID
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tomllib.TOMLDecodeError, TOMLDecodeError.msg, TOMLDecodeError.doc, TOMLDecodeError.pos, TOMLDecodeError.lineno, TOMLDecodeError.colno
  note    the message opens `line L, column C:`, the column in characters, then says what was expected and what was found; a byte order mark is refused, as tomllib refuses it

capability toml/depth  nesting bounded, so that no walk of a value runs out of stack
  heroes  toml.MAX_DEPTH
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    512 levels, the keys of a path and the arrays and inline tables around a value counted together; `parse` and `format` refuse a deeper one, where tomllib recurses until Python's own limit

capability toml/lookup  a value reached by a path of keys
  heroes  toml.value_at
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  dict.__getitem__, dict.get
  note    a key absent fails `not_found`, as does an index past an array; at an array a step is an index in decimal from "0", as `value_of` reads a document's path; a step into a value that is neither a table nor an array, or a step at an array that is no index, fails `invalid`

capability toml/write  a tree of values written as TOML text that reads back as the same tree
  heroes  toml.format
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    tomllib writes nothing; each table's plain keys first, then its tables under headers and its arrays of tables under `[[...]]`, every other value inline, a float in the fewest digits that read back; a date, a time or an offset outside its range fails `invalid`. TOML 1.1.0 since 2026-11-19: ESC written `\e` and the other control characters with no short escape `\xHH`, where `\u00HH` was written; a time with its seconds and an inline table on one line, which every version reads. Every valid case of toml-test's 1.1.0 list written back reads in tomllib 3.15.2 as the case does, 218 of 218

capability toml/comments-kept  a document's comments and layout kept from read to write
  heroes  toml.Document, toml.parse_document, toml.format_document
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    refused until 2026-11-17, when the author's decision (queue 58) asked for it: the package manager writes `heroes.toml` with it. The yardsticks are Rust's crate toml_edit (77th crate; DocumentMut, its parse and Display) and PyPI's tomlkit (53rd; parse, dumps, TOMLDocument), neither in a standard library. A document is held as its text and every edit rewrites only the bytes it touches; measured on 2026-11-17 over every TOML file the repository tracks, 232 of which 222 are TOML 1.0.0 (the other 10 refused by `parse` and `parse_document` alike), each written back byte for byte, and over the 45 TOML 1.0.0 inputs of this package's own tests; measured again on 2026-11-19 under TOML 1.1.0, the same 232 files: 222 read and written back byte for byte, the 10 refused (catalogues the compiler's tests write wrong on purpose, which tomllib 3.15.2 refuses too), 497 leaves set to themselves (493 byte for byte), 497 removed, 676 keys added, no failure

capability toml/edit-set  a value set by its path in a document edited in place, made where it is missing, every other byte kept
  heroes  toml.set_value, toml.add_table
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the yardsticks toml_edit (Table::insert, IndexMut, Item, value, table, InlineTable::insert) and tomlkit (TOMLDocument.__setitem__, Table.add, Table.append, tomlkit.table, tomlkit.inline_table), tomlkit 0.15.2 observed on 2026-11-17 from a copy in the scratch folder, toml_edit not on this machine. A value replaced keeps its line, its spacing and its comment; a new key goes after the last key line of its table, indented as it, before the blank lines and comments that close the table, where tomlkit puts it after those comments; a new table is a section after the last section of the table that holds it, a table's own header before its sub-tables, as tomlkit does both; a root with no key takes one before the comments that stand on its first header, where tomlkit puts it after them; CR LF documents get CR LF lines, where tomlkit writes LF; a member added to an inline table goes after the last, `{ x = 1, w = true }` where tomlkit writes `{ x = 1, w = true}`. Values in `format`'s forms; a value set to itself is written back byte for byte where the file used those forms (493 of 497 leaves of the repository's files; the 4 others a literal string, a string of three quotes, a raw tab and a `\u` escape, each rewritten in its own bytes). Every edit is read back and held to the same edit made on the tree, so none can leave text that reads otherwise; the repository's 222 files took a key added to each of their 676 tables, none failing

capability toml/edit-remove  a key, a value inside an array or an inline table, a table or an entry of an array of tables taken out of a document edited in place
  heroes  toml.remove_value
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the yardsticks toml_edit (Table::remove, Array::remove, ArrayOfTables::remove) and tomlkit (`del`, Container.remove, Array.pop); a key goes with its line and its trailing comment, never the comment above it, as tomlkit does; a table with its sections and the empty lines after them, the comments above the next header kept, where tomlkit takes them with the table; a table or an array that only the removed key made stays, `a.b = {}` or `key = []`, so that the tree is the tree with that one value gone, where tomlkit lets such tables vanish and puts `bin = []` at the top of the document; an item of an array written across lines takes its own line and comment, as tomlkit does. Every leaf of the repository's 222 files removed once, 497, none failing

capability toml/edit-array-of-tables  an entry appended to an array of tables in a document edited in place, its index answered
  heroes  toml.add_table_entry
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the yardsticks toml_edit (ArrayOfTables::push) and tomlkit (AoT.append); a section `[[...]]` after the array's last entry and that entry's sub-tables, a blank line kept before the section that follows, where tomlkit leaves none; an array written inline takes the entry as an inline table after its last item; an absent path makes the array

capability toml/edit-lookup  a value of a document edited in place read by a path of keys and array indices
  heroes  toml.value_of
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    the yardsticks toml_edit's Index (`doc["bin"][1]["name"]`) and tomlkit's `__getitem__`; a step at an array is an index in decimal from "0", no sign and no leading zero, so an entry of an array of tables is named as `["bin", "1", "name"]`, the shape of a JSON pointer's steps

capability toml/to-json  a TOML document or value crossed to a JSON value
  heroes  toml.to_json
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    added 2026-11-16 by the author's answer on serde, the part of it Heroes can say; tables become objects in their keys' order and arrays lists; a date or a time crosses as the RFC 3339 text `format` writes for it, `1979-05-27T07:32:00-07:00`, its kind lost on the way; a `nan` or an infinity fails `unsupported` and an integer past 2^53 - 1 `does_not_fit`, each with its JSON pointer, never rounded in silence

capability toml/from-json  a JSON value crossed to a TOML value, a document where it is an object
  heroes  toml.from_json
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    a whole number from -2^63 to 2^63 - 1 becomes an `integer`, every other number, `-0.0` included, a `float`; a string stays a `text` even where it reads as a date, since JSON names none; JSON's `null` fails `unsupported` with its JSON pointer, TOML having no such value

capability toml/records  a table filled into a record of the program by its field names, and a record written as a table
  heroes  refused: the language has no reflection; a program reads each value with `value_at` and builds its record by its `Record::field` names (spec § 9), which the compiler checks, as `msgpack`'s and `cbor`'s blocks of the same capability say; settings read in layers with typed getters are `config`'s
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -

dropped
  python  TOMLDecodeError.add_note, TOMLDecodeError.args, TOMLDecodeError.with_traceback: what every Python exception has
